    "text": "1891 Darker sequel printed in Ghent; asks whether reform or revenge can liberate a people. Printed in Ghent in 1891 with help from his friend Valentín Ventura. Rizal dedicated it to Mariano Gómez, José Burgos, and Jacinto Zamora, the three priests executed in 1872. The darker sequel asks whether reform is still possible after the failure of the Noli, and warns that revenge corrupts even those who seek freedom. Sourced text: Chapter I — On the Upper Deck Sic itur ad astra One morning in December the steamer Tabo was laboriously ascending the tortuous course of the Pasig, carrying a large crowd of passengers toward the province of La Laguna. She was a heavily built steamer, almost round, like the tabú from which she derived her name, quite dirty in spite of her pretensions to whiteness, majestic and grave from her leisurely motion. Altogether, she was held in great affection in that region, perhaps from her Tagalog name, or from the fact that she bore the characteristic impress of things in the country, representing something like a triumph over progress, a steamer that was not a steamer at all, an organism,…",
