    "type": "Work",
    "title": "A la Juventud Filipina",
    "text": "1879 Prize-winning call to the youth as “the fair hope of the fatherland.” Rizal wrote 'To the Filipino Youth' at eighteen for a contest held by the Liceo Artístico-Literario in Manila, and won first prize, a silver pen shaped like a feather. It calls on young Filipinos to use their talents, calling them 'the fair hope of the fatherland.' Sourced text: Hold high the brow serene, O youth, where now you stand; Let the bright sheen Of your grace be seen, Fair hope of my fatherland! Come now, thou genius grand, And bring down inspiration; With thy mighty hand, Swifter than the wind’s violation, Raise the eager mind to higher station. Come down with pleasing light Of art and science to the fight, O youth, and there untie The chains that heavy lie, Your spirit free to blight. See how in flaming zone Amid the shadows thrown, The Spaniard’s holy hand A crown’s resplendent band Proffers to this Indian land. Thou, who now wouldst rise On wings of rich emprise, Seeking from Olympian skies Songs of sweetest strain, Softer than ambrosial rain; Thou,…",
