    "type": "Work",
    "title": "A las flores de Heidelberg",
    "text": "1886 Asks flowers to carry his love home while he studied in Germany. Written in Heidelberg in the spring of 1886 while Rizal was training as an eye doctor in Germany. The poem asks flowers to carry his love home, giving a gentle voice to the homesickness of his years abroad. Sourced text: Go to my native land, go, foreign flowers, Sown by the traveler on his way; And there, beneath its azure sky, Where all of my affections lie; There from the weary pilgrim say, What faith is his in that land of ours! Go there and tell how when the dawn, Her early light diffusing, Your petals first flung open wide; His steps beside chill Neckar drawn, You see him silent by your side, Upon its Spring perennial musing. Saw how when morning’s light, All your fragrance stealing, Whispers to you as in mirth Playful songs of love’s delight, He, too, murmurs his love’s feeling In the tongue he learned at birth. That when the sun on Koenigstuhl’s height Pours out its golden flood, And with its slowly…",
