    "title": "Mi retiro",
    "text": "1895 Long meditation on retreat, work, and solitude, sent to his mother. A long poem written in Dapitan in 1895 and sent to his mother, Teodora. It describes his house by the sea and the peace he found there, along with the pain of distance. It is one of his most personal poems, showing how he made exile into a space for work and reflection. Sourced text: By the spreading beach where the sands are soft and fine, At the foot of the mount in its mantle of green, I have built my hut in the pleasant grove’s confine; From the forest seeking peace and a calmness divine, Rest for the weary brain and silence to my sorrow keen. Its roof the frail palm-leaf and its floor the cane, Its beams and posts of the unhewn wood; Little there is of value in this hut so plain, And better by far in the lap of the mount to have lain, By the song and the murmur of the high sea’s flood. A purling brook from the woodland glade Drops down o’er the stones and around it sweeps, Whence a fresh stream is drawn by the rough cane’s aid; That in the still night its murmur…",
